{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788975786,"nonce":"Fst7rWSJifPj/6Vehnio8KoLmcE=","title":"Bounds-check elimination needs induction-variable range facts","message":"HashLink puts array bounds checks in Haxe source, so they arrive as a bytecode\n`Field(length)` + unsigned compare + branch that AIR can see and remove.\n\n`RedundantGuardElim` (`passes/guardelim.rs`) removes a guard a dominating\nCondJump already proved. It is written and tested but NOT registered in the\npipeline, because on the benchmark corpus it fires twice -- both in deltablue,\nneither in nbody -- while costing ~4.8% of pipeline time.\n\nThe reason it cannot fire on nbody is the missing fact, not a missing\nunification: GVN already unifies the two `bodies.length` loads, so the loop guard\nand the bounds check compare the same two SSA values. But the loop guard is\nsigned (`i \u003cs size`) and the bounds check is unsigned (`i \u003cu size`), and the\nimplication needs `i \u003e=s 0`. That is an induction-variable range fact.\n\nWhat would make the pass earn its place: a phi whose preheader input is a\nnon-negative constant and whose latch input is `phi + positive constant`, where\nthe increment is dominated by the loop exit test on the same phi, is provably in\n`[0, N]`. nbody's inner loop starts at `i + 1`, so the analysis has to reach a\nfixpoint over derived start values, not just constants.","files":null}]}